 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Last video showed that six vector instructions can replace 578 scalar ones. Now we open the machine that runs them: the textbook's VMIPS, and its modern relative, the RISC-V vector extension."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Vector architecture"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the eight stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The machine, its vector registers, why they are registers at all, and the pipelined units."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Then the instruction set, VMIPS beside RISC-V, writing a small program, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=7]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "machine",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: VMIPS is an ordinary scalar MIPS processor with a vector unit added. Its heart is the vector register file."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 8: the VMIPS machine"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "dg"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.vr"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A vector load-store unit moves whole vectors between memory and the registers. It is pipelined too: after an initial latency it delivers one word per cycle."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.mem"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.ls",
       "delay": 0.3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Five vector functional units read from and write to the vector registers: floating-point add, multiply and divide, integer, and logical."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.add"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.mul",
       "delay": 0.15
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.div",
       "delay": 0.3
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.int",
       "delay": 0.45
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.log",
       "delay": 0.6
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And the scalar registers supply single values, like the a in DAXPY, and addresses."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dg.sr"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "memory ↔ load/store ↔ vector registers ↔ pipelined units"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "regs",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: VMIPS has eight vector registers, V0 to V7."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 8: vector registers"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "dg"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "vr"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "vr[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Each one holds 64 elements of 64 bits: a whole vector of doubles."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "vr[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Eight registers of 64 elements of 8 bytes: 4 kilobytes. Far bigger than a scalar register file of 32 registers."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "vr[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Several units work at once, so the file needs many ports: 16 read and 8 write, connected to the units by crossbars."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "vr[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Two special registers come up again and again: the vector length register, VLR, for vectors shorter than 64, and the vector mask register, VM, for conditionals. Each gets its own video."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "vr[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "8 registers × 64 elements × 64 bits = 4 KB"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "why",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: why keep vectors in registers at all? The earliest vector machines did not. The CDC STAR-100 of 1974 read every operand from memory and wrote every result back."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 8: why registers"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "vr"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "mm"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mm[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "That made every instruction wait for memory before its first element could start: a long startup on every operation."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mm[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And a result needed by the next instruction made a round trip through memory."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mm[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The Cray-1, two years later, put vectors in registers. Loads and stores move data in bulk, and arithmetic works register to register, exactly like a RISC processor. Every vector machine since has followed it, VMIPS included."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "mm[1][2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "load once, compute in registers, store once"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "pipe",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: each vector unit is fully pipelined. Here is a 4-stage adder working through a vector."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 4 of 8: pipelined units"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "mm"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "pipe"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pipe[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Element 0 enters in cycle 1, element 1 in cycle 2, and so on. A new element starts every cycle."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pipe[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The first result appears after the pipeline latency, in cycle 5. After that, one result comes out every cycle."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "pipe[4]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The pipeline never stalls inside a vector, because the elements are independent. So vector units can be very deeply pipelined, and clocked fast. A vector of n elements takes about the latency plus n cycles."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "pipe[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "one element per cycle, after the startup latency"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "isa",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: the instruction set. Arithmetic comes in two forms. Vector-vector: ADDVV.D adds two vectors element by element."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 8: the instruction set"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "pipe"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "isa"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Vector-scalar: ADDVS.D adds the same scalar to every element."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Three kinds of load and store. Unit stride, LV and SV, for consecutive elements."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Strided, LVWS, for every k-th element: a column of a matrix stored by rows."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And indexed, LVI, which loads from addresses given by another vector: a gather, for sparse data."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[4]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Compares set bits in the mask register, and MTC1 sets the vector length. These come later in the module."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "isa[5..=6]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "VV and VS arithmetic · unit, strided and indexed memory"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "rv",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 6: the same operations in the RISC-V vector extension, which real chips now ship."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 6 of 8: VMIPS and RISC-V V"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "isa"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rv"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "vsetvli sets the vector length and the element width."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rv[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Loads: vle64 for unit stride, vlse64 for strided, vluxei64 for indexed."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rv[1..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Arithmetic: vfmul dot vf multiplies a vector by a scalar register, and vfadd dot vv adds two vectors. The same pattern as MULVS and ADDVV."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rv[4..=5]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And vse64 stores."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rv[6]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The big difference: RISC-V does not fix the vector length at 64. Each chip chooses its own, and vsetvli tells the program how many elements fit."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "rv"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "rvx"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rvx[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The element width is chosen at run time too, so the same registers hold 64-bit doubles or 8-bit pixels."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "rvx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So one binary runs on a small chip with short vectors and a big chip with long ones."
